    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 91,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports a layered state-space conclusion. Under exact repetition, nominally identical questions were stable at the semantic-class level across all 16 anchors, so meaningful semantic instability is not supported; however, broader observable response-state variation can still occur. Prompt paraphrasing and prior-answer context reliably increased full-state distance without producing semantic flips, instead altering dimensions such as claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. Prior history also produced measurable claim and action flips. Thus, explanations based on pervasive semantic randomness should be rejected, while explanations emphasizing condition-sensitive movement within a stable semantic region should be retained. Interpretation should prioritize semantic class and action, which were measured reliably; claims about claim state, abstention, exact referents, and exact evidence sets require greater caution because their scoring reliability was weaker."
